From f70d16fb33f3eab84b5333d18ee5d7193dba14f6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrei Gosman <andrei.gosman@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 11:40:05 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] support: emulate timerfd with kqueue and a pipe on Darwin

io_timer_source creates a periodic timerfd and registers it in the
io_broker, reading the expiration count out of the descriptor on every
tick. Darwin has no timerfd and no POSIX interval timers, so neither
timerfd_create nor itimerspec exists there.

Add timerfd_compat.h/.cpp next to io_timer_source. On Linux the header is
a drop-in for <sys/timerfd.h>. On Darwin a process-wide kqueue drives the
timing with EVFILT_TIMER, one background thread waits on it, and each
timer hands the caller the read end of a pipe into which the thread
writes the expiration count. The descriptor is readable and pollable, so
io_timer_source keeps its logic unchanged; only the include moves.

Two details to know. Linux coalesces expirations into one read(); here
each wake-up writes its own count, so a slow reader sees several
eight-byte values whose sum is the same, which the tick loop handles.
And because callers close the descriptor with plain close(), dead
entries are reclaimed at the next timerfd_create, before pipe(), so a
reused descriptor number cannot match a stale entry. The write end has
F_SETNOSIGPIPE so a closed reader cannot kill the process.

Same design as a prior srsRAN 4G Darwin port, verified there
for periodic delivery through read(), one-shot delivery through poll(),
disarm and timerfd_gettime.
